# Without this file, `COPY . /app` copied the whole working directory into the image.
#
# That mattered because the README tells the reader to create `.env_file` holding
# JWT_SECRET, and then to `docker build` in the same directory. .gitignore keeps that file
# out of git; nothing kept it out of the image. Measured by building and inspecting:
#
#   /app/.env_file:
#   JWT_SECRET='MyJWTTTT'
#   .git present: 28 files
#
# buildspec.yml pushes that image to ECR, so the secret travelled to a registry and sat in
# a layer readable by anyone who could pull it. Deleting the file in a later layer would
# not have helped either, since earlier layers keep it.
.env
.env.*
.env_file
.flaskenv
.envrc
.direnv
*.pem
*.key
credentials*
secrets/
.aws/
parameters.json

# Version control and CI metadata: not needed at runtime, and .git carries the full history.
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github/
.circleci/

# Infrastructure and docs: deployed by other means, or not deployed at all.
README.md
*.cfn.yml
buildspec.yml
simple_jwt_api.yml
iam-role-policy.json
trust.json
parameters.example.json

# Tests and their artifacts. The image serves the app; buildspec runs pytest before the
# build, from the source checkout rather than from inside the image.
test_main.py
.pytest_cache/
htmlcov/
.coverage
.coverage.*
.tox/

# Build and editor noise.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.egg
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/
env/
.idea/
.DS_Store
